I'll read the Investigation playbook and `calc.js` to answer this.The function is a bare `a + b`. I'll confirm the runtime behavior with node, without writing any files.`add("2", "3")` returns the string `"23"`, not the number `5`. It joins the two strings together instead of adding them.

```1:3:calc.js
export function add(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}
```

The function is a bare `+` with no conversion or validation. In JavaScript, `+` concatenates whenever either operand is a string. It only adds numerically when both operands are numbers. I ran the real module in node to confirm. `add("2", "3")` gave `"23"` with type `string`. Mixed input behaves the same way, so `add(2, "3")` also gives `"23"`. Only `add(2, 3)` gives `5`.

One thing to watch. Any caller that passes raw form input or query-string values will get concatenated strings back, with no error. If you want numeric addition, convert at the boundary where the input arrives, for example with `Number(...)`, or have `add` reject non-numbers. That would be a separate change, and I made none. `git status` was clean after the check.

I answered this directly instead of handing it to the explainer subagent the investigation playbook would normally start. A three-line function plus one runtime check was enough evidence.
